    "text": "The dispatch office hummed with the low, constant breath of the building\u2019s ventilation system. It was a sound Sal had long ago stopped hearing, just as he no longer noticed the faint, metallic tang of ozone from the old relay boards or the way the fluorescent lights above his desk cast a sickly, greenish pallor on everything. The night was a long, dark river, and he was its quiet ferryman, guiding trains through the black with the push of a button and a voice over the radio.\n\nHis world was a horseshoe-shaped desk of worn Formica, cluttered with timetables, dispatch logs, and a cold cup of coffee. Outside his window, the rail yard was a study in black and silver, tracks gleaming under the security lights like the ribs of some colossal, sleeping beast.\n\nThe shift had been uneventful. The 11:45 freight to Chicago was running on time, the 12:10 local was tucked in for the night, and the 2:30 express was still just a blip on his screen, a hundred miles out. To pass the time between radio checks, he\u2019d been reorganizing the bookshelf. It was a task born of pure boredom, a way to keep his hands busy while his mind drifted.\n\nHe pulled down a thick, dog-eared volume from 1988. The cover was a faded, institutional green, the gold lettering almost completely worn away. It was a timetable for a route that no longer existed, a line that had been decommissioned before he\u2019d even started working",
